  "findings": [],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "topic": "Root versus storage ID",
      "result": "Node::account and Node::fork_with_lift compute mathematical path/depth/digest before an independent storage-record ID. Child IDs feed only encoded record pointers; no root digest or lift uses them. The replaced production entry previously called build_with_lift with db=None; RootDigest has no ID, Kind, encoder or database. Persistent Node and graph builders remain present.",
      "refs": [
        "account_archive:276-327",
        "account_archive:1047-1051",
        "account_archive:1201-1232",
        "native_root_digest:14-42"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Complete input and duplicate path work",
      "result": "ordered_accounts remains byte-identical, traverses all owners, hashes every path, sorts and rejects colliding paths. The new leaf uses that same-call path instead of hashing the owner a second time; leaf hash still binds exact owner, balance and nonce. No touched-set or authority cache is introduced.",
      "refs": [
        "account_archive:210-232",
        "account_archive:1024-1034",
        "native_root_digest:46-83"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Fork and lift equivalence",
      "result": "The five fork short-circuit checks are text-identical and precede both child lifts. Old lift body equals extracted lift_digest after only replacing self fields with parameters and whitespace. LiftCache retains exact path/digest/source-depth/target-depth tuple, exact equality after bucket indexing, identity case, 8192 limit and same replacement algorithm.",
      "refs": [
        "native_root_digest:25-42",
        "account_archive:239-255",
        "native_store:24-142"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Depth and cancellation order",
      "result": "Empty input returns before callback; each nonempty subtree calls progress before leaf/depth validation, then recurses left, right, fork in the original order. Production root entries both use a no-op callback. Stored paths retain original progress checks. This does not add cancellation capability or prove equal wall-clock cancellation outcomes.",
      "refs": [
        "native_root_digest:46-83",
        "account_archive:1201-1232",
        "native_store:476-536"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "SQL and Host boundary",
      "result": "Both compared files retain identical SQL literal lines. Every byte of native_store after LiftCache is unchanged (39376 bytes), including seed, apply, verify, graph walk, current-account projection and child validation. No Host, resource, transaction-commit or service policy is added to the new digest module. This statement is limited to these reviewed changes, not unrelated changes in the same commit.",
      "refs": [
        "native_store:145-1159",
        "native_root_digest:1-89"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Tests inspected, not executed",
      "result": "Three new non-ignored tests compare record-building and independent sparse roots across 0/1/2/3/17/65/513 accounts and owner/balance/nonce changes; compare 35 cancellation positions for valid/repeated/reversed rows; compare six fork variants and boundary lift depths. One explicitly ignored cost observation preserves create-new output and source hashes, two cold plus 100 warm observations per arm/size, with business qualification false. No actual test result is inferred.",
      "refs": [
        "native_root_digest_tests:1-311"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "important_production_invariant": {
    "statement": "RootDigest::build is private and production root() passes only ordered_accounts(values). RootDigest leaves trust rows[0].0 == path(rows[0].1).",
    "limit": "Arbitrary malformed internal tuples with an owner/path mismatch are not generally equivalent to old Node::account, which recomputes path(owner). The supplied production route cannot provide such tuples; this is not a reachable production blocker or a claim of equivalence for every possible private build argument."
  },
